Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: The Turntable Swallows Fog
- Story & Context: Recorded during an unauthorized midnight hookup into the railway junction power grid at the Richards Bay Coal Terminal, the track captures the exact moment a runaway shunting locomotive sheared through an automated hydraulic barrier, spraying sparks onto Sipho’s open reel deck. The team left the recording unedited, using the rhythmic metal-on-metal screeches of the frozen rail turntable to trigger their massive sub-bass horns against the cold marine fog rolling off the Indian Ocean. Mira leaned into a broadcast megaphone to deliver the opening litany, singing: "Listen to the weight of the iron talking to the water."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A massive railway turntable slowly rotating through a dense, blue-gray ocean fog, its steel tracks glowing dull orange from friction, while distant cargo train silhouettes loom like sleeping leviathans under an overcast sky filled with dim orbital navigation beacons.

Track 2: Kolekar's Last Manifest
- Story & Context: Written in memory of Anand Kolekar, an eccentric Goa harbor-master who walked off the pier at Vasco da Gama with only an encrypted radio transponder in his pocket, this confession piece reconstructs his final radio transmissions against weeping, microtonal sitar glissandos. In the control room of a grounded ore ship, Mira fed Kolekar's voice through a modified spring reverb tank filled with freezing seawater, allowing his crackling confessions to be swallowed whole by sudden, percussive Amapiano log-bass thuds. As the low-end foghorn pulse answered his final recorded sigh, Kolekar’s voice broke through the speaker static with his final message: "Clear the track, the last departure carries no cargo."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: An abandoned dockmaster's ticket booth at the end of a wet wooden pier, glass cracked and covered in sea-spray frost, an analog timetable board flapping violently in the wind with blurred Sanskrit-English flight and train schedules, illuminated only by a single amber sodium bulb.

Track 3: Side-Chain Shunt (Terminal 9)
- Story & Context: The track was born when a three-day freight backlog at Terminal 9 forced six dry-docked container ships to blow their emergency horns simultaneously to stave off navigational panic in zero-visibility fog, creating an accidental raga in Bhairavi that vibrated the concrete foundations of the coastal railway depot. The band captured the chaotic drone using submerged hydrophones placed in flooded switchyard trenches, chaining the raw ship sirens to a galloping, 303-driven Goa psytrance bassline that accelerates and decelerates like an overloaded train failing to brake on icy rails. Over the climactic swell of acid squelches and clattering rail couplings, an automated station announcer chant emerges through the thunder: "All tracks terminate in open water."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: An apocalyptic coastal train station submerged in knee-deep tidal waters, where wet railway tracks vanish straight into a vaporous ocean horizon, overhead catenary wires crackling with electric-blue psytrance energy while towering ship bows emerge out of the misty sky like ghosts.

